lower: "Attention shape strategy: dense grid today, and planned flash attention" {
  style.fill: "#EEF1F8"
  style.stroke: "#CFD2DD"
  style.font-color: "#212121"
  style.font-size: 20
  style.bold: true
  style.border-radius: 10
  label.near: top-left
  grid-rows: 2
  grid-columns: 1
  grid-gap: 26

  strategies: " " {
    style.fill: "#EEF1F8"
    style.stroke: "#EEF1F8"
    grid-rows: 1
    grid-columns: 2
    grid-gap: 26

    # A markdown-labelled shape gets neither a border nor a width from D2, so the
    # border and the width live on a wrapping container and the prose sits inside
    # it as a blended child.
    dense: "A · Dense grid, the strategy shown above" {
      style.fill: "#E3E9FD"
      style.stroke: "#0d47a1"
      style.stroke-width: 2
      style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
      style.font-size: 15
      style.bold: true
      style.border-radius: 8
      label.near: top-left
      grid-rows: 1
      grid-columns: 1
      grid-gap: 0
      width: 700

      dense_body: |md
        Pack the ragged flat list into a rectangle, so every  
        shape is fixed at compile time.  
        Needed because an offset computed from data does not  
        lower under compilation (torch-spyre#3508) — exactly  
        the arithmetic that flash relies on.  
        Eager tolerates it; compiled mode, our target, does not.

        · build an additive mask [S, 1, L, L] marking padding  
        and cross-request slots  
        · build three index lists, then gather Q / K / V into  
        [S, H, L, D]  
        · one fused SDPA kernel per (S, L), then gather the  
        results back out

        **Shape axes: T for the body, (S, L) for attention.  
        Two sets of buckets to warm.**

        Costs: the mask grows with L², plus index lists and a  
        barrier per gather.  
        Works today. This is the current implementation.
      | {
        style.fill: "#E3E9FD"
        style.stroke: "#E3E9FD"
        style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
        style.font-size: 14
        width: 620
      }
    }

    flash: "B · Flash-style attention, planned" {
      style.fill: "#E8F5E9"
      style.stroke: "#2e7d32"
      style.stroke-width: 2
      style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
      style.font-size: 15
      style.bold: true
      style.border-radius: 8
      label.near: top-left
      grid-rows: 1
      grid-columns: 1
      grid-gap: 0
      width: 700

      flash_body: |md
        Never materialise the rectangle.  
        Each block of queries reads its request's offset from  
        the metadata at runtime, and works directly on the flat  
        list, carrying a running softmax maximum and sum.  
        That is the online-softmax structure the Spyre decoder  
        already uses over KV pages.

        · no [S, 1, L, L] mask; request boundaries are the offsets  
        · no gather_pack / gather_unpack; the kernel reads the  
        flat list directly  
        · no per-layer index lists, so no per-gather barriers

        **Shape axis: block count, bucketed like the decoder's  
        page loop. L disappears.**

        Both upstream backends already work this way, neither  
        padding into a rectangle.  
        Flash attention consumes the flat list with  
        cu_seqlens_q = query_start_loc, and the CPU backend  
        passes query[:num_actual_tokens] to a C++ kernel.
      | {
        style.fill: "#E8F5E9"
        style.stroke: "#E8F5E9"
        style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
        style.font-size: 14
        width: 620
      }
    }
  }

  footer: " " {
    style.fill: "#EEF1F8"
    style.stroke: "#EEF1F8"
    grid-rows: 1
    grid-columns: 2
    grid-gap: 26

    simplifies: "If flash attention lands, the design converges on upstream" {
      style.fill: "#FFF8E1"
      style.stroke: "#F9A825"
      style.stroke-width: 2
      style.font-color: "#5d4037"
      style.font-size: 15
      style.bold: true
      style.border-radius: 8
      label.near: top-left
      grid-rows: 1
      grid-columns: 1
      grid-gap: 0
      width: 1000

      simplifies_body: |md
        · the second shape axis collapses; bucket T and a block count,  
        much closer to upstream's single axis  
        · the batch-shape tables box disappears, and with it the  
        per-layer rebuild problem and its barriers  
        · gather_pack / SDPA / gather_unpack collapse into one  
        blocked-attention kernel  
        · the mask's L² growth at long prompts disappears, today's  
        largest untested risk

        **Open question:** whether a Spyre kernel can do the runtime  
        offset arithmetic this depends on. If it cannot, the block  
        count stays bucketed.  
        Everything else holds either way: padding seam, unpadded  
        metadata, warmup, compilation mode.
      | {
        style.fill: "#FFF8E1"
        style.stroke: "#FFF8E1"
        style.font-color: "#5d4037"
        style.font-size: 14
        width: 920
      }
    }

    legend: "Legend" {
      style.fill: "#FFFFFF"
      style.stroke: "#CFD2DD"
      style.font-color: "#212121"
      style.font-size: 20
      style.bold: true
      style.border-radius: 10
      label.near: top-left
      grid-rows: 5
      grid-columns: 1
      grid-gap: 6

      L1: "Upstream vLLM, used as-is" {
        style.fill: "#37474f"
        style.stroke: "#263238"
        style.font-color: "#ffffff"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

      L2: "spyre-inference component" {
        style.fill: "#1565c0"
        style.stroke: "#0d47a1"
        style.font-color: "#ffffff"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

      L3: "Internal step · dense-grid path" {
        style.fill: "#E3E9FD"
        style.stroke: "#0d47a1"
        style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

      L4: "Planned flash-attention path" {
        style.fill: "#E8F5E9"
        style.stroke: "#2e7d32"
        style.font-color: "#0A0F25"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

      L5: "Note or upstream dependency" {
        style.fill: "#FFF8E1"
        style.stroke: "#F9A825"
        style.font-color: "#5d4037"
        style.font-size: 14
        style.bold: false
        style.border-radius: 6
        width: 340
      }

    }
  }
}
